    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (74.49) and **Lawrence Butler** (69.79) anchor our offensive strategy as our highest-graded options. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a strong park factor and a favorable matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a, though we are monitoring the catcher\u2019s arm efficiency as a minor drag on his stolen base potential. Meanwhile, **Lawrence Butler** is in a prime spot against Taj Bradley; his +13% platoon edge and the park factor in his favor make him a must-start. **Jonathan Aranda** (66.84) also grades out as an elite play today, as his favorable matchup against Jackson Jobe\u2014coupled with a significant platoon advantage\u2014provides one of the best efficiency-to-roster-cost ratios on the team.\n\nWe are carefully navigating a few close calls in the outfield and utility slots. **Daulton Varsho** (62.56) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (61.29) are both strong starts, though we are keeping a close eye on the official lineups, as \"Lineup Pending\" status introduces variance for our lower-order bats. Our most difficult bench decision involved **Justin Crawford** (50.89); while he remains a critical asset due to his elite speed and potential in our 5x5 format, he missed the cut behind the superior platoon and order-position profiles of our current starters. The optimizer favored the higher lineup spots held by **Gleyber Torres** and **Zack Gelof** to maximize volume, even though **Justin Crawford** offers a higher ceiling in the scarcity-heavy stolen base category.\n\nRisks remain present across the board, particularly with the \"Lineup Pending\" status for almost every player, which forces us to rely on historical batting order assumptions. **Cal Raleigh** (49.78) and **Yainer Diaz** (51.46) are functioning as marginal starts; their scores are dampened by tough matchups against Aaron Nola and Will Warren, respectively, and we are managing their output to preserve our seasonal game caps. Finally, while **Bryce Harper** (61.17) draws a challenging opponent in George Kirby, his underlying xwOBA of .390 provides enough confidence to keep him in the utility role, provided the weather and confirmed lineups do not trigger a late-afternoon pivot.",
